   "is_new": true,
   "source": {
    "label": "thm:MatchingFC",
    "number": "Theorem 9",
    "quote": "If $\\alpha=\\sigma_1/(\\sigma_1 + \\sigma_2)$, the $\\alpha$-elimination strategy using the exploration rate $\\beta(t,\\delta)=\\log\\frac{t}{\\delta} + 2 \\log\\log(6t)$ is $\\delta$-PAC on $\\cM$ and satisfies, for every $\\nu\\in\\cM$, for every $\\epsilon>0$, $$\\bE_\\nu[\\tau] \\leq (1+\\epsilon)\\frac{2(\\sigma_1 + \\sigma_2)^2}{(\\mu_1-\\mu_2)^2}\\log\\left(\\frac{1}{\\delta}\\right) + \\underset{\\delta \\rightarrow 0}{o_{\\epsilon}}\\left(\\log\\left(\\frac{1}{\\delta}\\right)\\right).$$"
   },
   "confidence": "medium",
   "notes": "Delta = |mu1-mu2|. The o_eps(log 1/delta) term encoded via limsup notation (limsup E[tau]/log(1/delta) <= expression/log(1/delta)). Also delta-PAC. Together with Theorem 6 gives kappa_C = 2(sigma1+sigma2)^2/Delta^2. Paper says it also extends to sigma_a^2-subgaussian arms."
  },
